    "result_text": "The acceptance suite is the entire test set, and it's fully green (21/21, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization applied whenever an owner is created, satisfying the spec and all existing/new tests.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 now normalizes the address (`owner.setAddress(normalizeAddress(...))`) *before* the household duplicate check and before storing, so the normalized form is what gets persisted, returned, and compared.\n\n2. **New `normalizeAddress` method** \u2014 trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands common street-type abbreviations token-wise (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`) via an `ADDRESS_ABBREVIATIONS` map. It throws `InvalidAddressException` (400) when the address is blank after normalization. Example: `\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`.\n\n3. **`InvalidAddressException`** (new) + handler in **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 mirrors the existing `InvalidEmailException`/`InvalidTelephoneException` pattern, returning 400 Bad Request with structured validation details.\n\n**Why the comparisons are now consistent:** because every owner's address is stored in its canonical normalized form at creation, both comparison sites already operate on normalized values \u2014 household duplicate detection (`rejectDuplicateHousehold` via `Households.normalize` over the stored addresses) and the shared `householdId` (computed by `OwnerMapper` from `owner.getAddress()`). So `\"12 Main St\"`, `\"12 main street\"`, and `\"12  MAIN  ST\"` all collapse to the same household. I updated the `rejectDuplicateHousehold` javadoc to reflect that addresses are compared in normalized form.\n\nNote: the generated DTO already carries a `.*\\S.*` pattern on `address`, which independently rejects whitespace-only input with 400; the new `InvalidAddressException` covers the \"blank after normalization\" requirement explicitly at the service layer as defense-in-depth.",
